description.narrative:
Reform area: Reform Area 2: Improving Governance, Advancing Transparency and Driving Digital Transformation 

Outcome: The number of active e-commerce businesses increased to 6. (2022 baseline: 4) 
(Source: MEDT, TAJSTAT). 

Description: 1.1	To optimize and strengthen the overall digital transformation and e-commerce in Tajikistan, the government:
(i)	operationalized the Agency for Innovation and Digital Technologies created in 2021 by appointing the Chairman of the Agency, approved regulations of the Agency indicating structure, responsibilities, and staffing; and allocated funds totaling TJS4.0 million for the agency for FY2024; and

(ii)	approved the E-Commerce Program (2025-2029) for Tajikistan and Action Plan (2025-2027), covering 
(a)	interagency coordination;
(b)	strengthening e-government services for provision of public services to the citizens;
(c)	legislative framework for data security and data management;
(d)	consumer protection;
(e)	intellectual property rights;
(f)	incentives for SMEs related to e-commerce; and
(g)	strengthening the capacity of women entrepreneurs though a dedicated action plan.

Reform area: Reform Area 1: Strengthening Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises and Fostering Green Employment 

Outcome: Number of Presidential grants for women entrepreneurs increased from 80 recipients to 120 (2022 baseline: 80)
(Source: MOF data). 

Description: 2.2	(i)	To drive environmentally sustainable economic growth and attract investment, the Government enacted a decree operationalizing the Law on Organic Production, which includes:
(a)	objectives and principles of the organic production; 
(b)	conditions on the use of wild plants and their fruits in organic production; 
(c)	requirement for organic production; 
(d)	labelling, import and export of organic product;
(e)	transparency and disclosure of the information about organic production; and
(f)	dispute resolution mechanisms.

(ii)	 The MEDT established a high-level Steering Committee for the implementation of Green Taxonomies, and appointed at least three women members to ensure inclusive and gender-responsive decision-making.

Reform area: Reform Area 1: Strengthening Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises and Fostering Green Employment 

Outcome: Number of Presidential grants for women entrepreneurs increased from 80 recipients to 120 (2022 baseline: 80)
(Source: MOF data). 

Description: 2.4	To incentivize climate-friendly private sector financing to promote growth and employment, the MOF through a ministerial order, designated Main Department of Public Debt and Attracting Public Investments, to prepare and submit amendments to the Government, including the following key provisions:
(i)	Clear legal definition and classification of leasing arrangements, improving the enforceability of leasing contracts under civil and commercial law;
(ii)	Assigning the regulatory responsibilities ensuring alignment with the international practices;
(iii)	Mandatory application of IFRS for leasing companies to enhance financial transparency, reporting, and risk management;
(iv)	Clarification of rights and obligations of lessors and lessees, including provisions for dispute resolution and asset recovery; and
(v)	Strengthened provisions on prudential supervision and licensing, delegated to the regulator.
